{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target asserts anthropogenic heat flux is the primary nocturnal UHI driver, but the dominant textbook mechanism for nighttime UHI is the slow release of heat stored during the day in urban thermal mass (asphalt, concrete, buildings). The target even acknowledges this stored-heat mechanism in its counterconditions, and under clear, calm rural conditions the countryside cools rapidly via longwave radiative loss while the city's thermal mass retards cooling. Anthropogenic heat is typically a secondary contribution to the nocturnal energy budget in most cities, not the primary cause."}]}